 is is the fabled evaluation  function but instead of programming it  directly  we got the system to learn it for itself  so I haven't got time to go into the  technical details but if you're  interested you can read our nature front  cover article on this that came out  earlier this year sorry last year so we  took this system and we wanted to test  it bring it to the ultimate sort of  challenge and in March of last year we  took on a legend of the game  the south korean grandmaster lee sedol  he's won 18 world titles in his widely  acknowledged to be the greatest player  of the past decade and we played this  match in seoul five-game match for a  million dollars first prize and the  whole of korea basically  came to Santa chol at one point every  national TV broadcaster was broadcasting  the match live all all of the different  TV channels so we played this game is  amazing once-in-a-lifetime experience  and we managed to win to everyone's  surprise the match for one and this was  described and proclaimed there's a  decade before its time from the AI  experts and the NGO experts and that's  very unusual for all of you involved in  technology for something to happen a  decade before it was predicted in  technology and engineering but more  importantly than the fact that alphago  won this match was how it won and I just  want to take a moment to try and explain  to you some of the innovations that  alphago came up with so in the game of  Go there are every now and again every  sort of twenty years as famous games are  played where a move so astounding as  played is recorded for all history is  written about in the in the go books and  it's sort of regarded as a sort of  mystical move and I think in this  five-game match there were several moves  like that but this is my favorite one  this is a position from game 2 and this  is mu 37 alphago is black and Lisa Dahl  is white and alphago played this move  here on the right hand side marked by  the white triangle so it's called a  shoulder hit because it's off to the  side of that white stone now this move  were totally unthinkable to all the  professionals in the NGO world and I  just want to take a moment to try and  explain to you why this move was so  creative and so unthinkable in the game  of Go there are two important lines on  this 19 by 19 board there's the third  line here now if you play a stone on the  third line what you're saying to your  opponent is you're trying to surround  territory to the side of the board so  this is how you get points at the end of  the game so you're trying to claim  territory instead of that if you play on  the fourth line what you're saying to  your ponent is you can take territory on  the third line but I'm going to take  power and influence into the center of  the board and the idea of playing on the  fourth line is that later on in the game  that power and influence will translate  into points and territory somewhere else  on  bored now for three thousand years of  human study and contemplating this game  the third and fourth lines were  considered to be equal so if you play in  the third line your opponent plays in  the fourth line that's more or less an  equal exchange but instead of that  alphago played on the fifth line and  this is completely still thought to be  an unthinkable idea because if you play  on the fifth line you're giving your  opponent territory from the fourth line  so they're getting much more territory  for your influence but alphago seems to  like this move and moves like this so it  may be that for three thousand years  humans have been under estimating the  power and the use of this influence  towards the center of the board and sort  of underrating its value so later on the  interesting thing about go is that it's  considered to be more than just the game  in in China and Japan and Korea it's  considered to be more like an art form  something more akin to poetry in fact  Confucius wrote about go as one of the  f